    "ls|LS17_S3_U_251": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH & EDUCATION \n \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 251 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04/02/2020 \n \n \nSPENDING ON AGRICULTURAL RESEARCH \n \n251. SHRI RAJENDRA AGRAWAL:  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE    \nd`f\"k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the amount of money spent on funding agricultural research in terms of GDP \ncontribution; \n \n(b) whether the Government plans to increase it further and if so, the details thereof and \nif not, the reasons therefor; \n \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to improve the quality of agricultural research and \ninfrastructure in the existing bodies; and  \n \n(d) whether the Government is undertaking foreign collaboratio ns with think tanks and \nresearch institutions to improve quality of agricultural research, and if so, the details \nthereof? \nA N S W E R \n \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nd`f”k vkSj fdlku dY;k.k ea=h                           (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a) The amount of money spent on agricultural research and education in terms of \npercentage of Agriculture Gross Domestic Product (Agri -GDP) in India was 0.60% during \n2017-18 at current prices.  \n \n(b) The public budget for agricultural research and education increased from Rs \n102.1 billion in 2014-15 to Rs. 111.21 billion in 2017-18. \n \n(c) The steps to improve the quality of agricultural research and infrastructures are taken \nup on the recommendations of the subject specific expert committees. The  Expenditure \nFinance Committee (EFC)/ Standing Finance Committee (SFC) approves such \nrecommendations for improving quality of agricultural research and infrastructure in \nexisting bodies. \n \n(d) The Department of Agricultural Research & Education (DARE), Gove rnment of \nIndia has been collaborating with foreign research institutions/ universities in all the fields \nof agriculture to improve quality of research outputs. Presently, DARE/ICAR has 59 active \nMoUs for collaboration with foreign organizations including Universities, CGIAR \ninstitutions, FAO and other international institutions. \n******* ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_252": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RAILWAYS \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED  QUESTION NO. 252 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11.03.2020 \n \nNON-RAILWAY OFFICERS IN RAILWAY BOARD \n \n*252.SHRI VELUSAMY P.: \n  \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n \n \n(a) whether the Government is having any proposal to bring non -\nrailway officers in the Railway Board; and  \n  \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF RAILWAYS AND COMMERCE & INDUSTRY \n(SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n(a) and (b): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n***** \n \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) AND (b) OF \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 252 BY SHRI VELUSAMY P.  TO BE \nANSWERED IN LOK SABHA ON 11.03.2020 REGARDING  NON-RAILWAY \nOFFICERS IN RAILWAY BOARD \n \n(a)  & (b):  At present, the following posts of Railway Board are filled \nup, by non-railway officers, either on deputation basis or against \nencadred post of the respective Ministries:- \n \nS. No. Name of Post \n1. Director General, Railway Protection Force(Indian Police \nService) \n2. Director, Vigilance Police (Indian Police Service) \n3. Additional Director General/Public Relations (Indian \nInformation Service) \n4. Senior Economic Adviser(Indian Economic Service) \n5. Economic Adviser(Indian Economic Service) \n6. Director, Economic Unit(Indian Economic Service) \n7. Deputy Director, Economic Unit (Indian Economic \nService) \n8. Legal Adviser(Indian Legal Service) \n \n**** ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_253": " \n \n                                  \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n253\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n4\nTH\n \nFEBRUARY\n,\n \n20\n20\n \n \nBENEFICIARIES\n \nOF PM KISAN SCHEME\n \n \n253.\n \n \nDR. SHASHI THAROOR:\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n(a)\n \n \nwhether the Pradhan Mantri \n  \nKisan Samman Nidhi (PM\n-\nKISAN) scheme aims to give \nRs. 6,000 per year to about 12.8 crore eligible small and marginal farmers;\n \n \n(b)\n \n \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(c) \n \nwheth\ner only around 8 crore beneficiaries have been paid the first instalment so far, \nleaving around 5.8 crore out of the net, if so, the details thereof; \n \n \n(d) \n \nthe steps taken by the Government to quicken farmer data validation process including \nupdating of l\nand database; \n \n \n(e)\n \n \nwhether benefits will be distributed to eligible beneficiaries with retrospective effect, if so, \ndetails thereof, State/UT\n-\nwise; and \n \n \n(f)\n \n \nif not, the reasons therefor?\n \n \n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nM\nINISTER \n \nOF AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान क\u0000याण \nमं\u0000ी\n \n \n   \n(\nSHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR\n)\n \n \n(a) & \n(b)\n: \n \nYes, Sir. \nUnder PM\n-\nKISAN Scheme funds of Rs.6000/\n-\n \nper annum per \nbeneficiary are transferred to the accounts of beneficiaries in three equal installments, \nevery four month\ns\n \nafter rec\neipt of verification of beneficiaries from concerned State/UT \nand their validation th\nrough Aadhar/PFMS. The\n \nnumber of landholding\ns\n \nfor \ntransfer of \nbenefit is\n \nto be about 14 crore.\n \n \n(c)\n \n& \n(\nd)\n:   Registration of farmers for this scheme and subsequent install\nment\n-\nwise \ntransfer of benefits to them is a continuous and ongoing process.The transfer of benefits \nto the identified eligible beneficiaries takes place as and when their correct and verified \ndata are uploaded by the concerned State / UT Governments on PM\n-\nKisan web\n-\nportal \nwww.pmkisan.gov.in. The speed with which error\n-\nfree beneficiaries’ data is \nuploaded \ndiffers from State to State. The West Bengal Government has not yet decided to join the \nscheme. \n \n \n \nContd…2/\n-\n \n \n\n-\n2\n-\n \n \nThe data of beneficiaries so uploaded by\n \nthe State / UT Governments undergoes a multi\n-\nlevel verification and validation by various concerned agencies, including the banks, which \nincludes rejection of data for errors at various levels and re\n-\nuploading of error\n-\nfree data by the \nState / UT Governme\nnts. Thereafter only the amount is successfully transferred into the bank \naccounts of the beneficiaries. This entire process is replicated for transfer of every installment\n \n \n           \nAs on 30.01.2020, the benefits have been released to 8,35,77,649 farmer\ns.\n \n \n \n      \n \nTo further facilitate registration of farmers, an exclusive ‘Farmers Corner’ has been \nprovided in the PM\n-\nKISAN Web\n-\nportal through which the farmers can do their self\n-\nregistration. \nFarmers can also edit their names in PM\n-\nKisan database as per th\neir Aadhaar Card through the \nFarmers Corner. Farmers can also know the status of their payment through the Farmers \nCorner. The village\n-\nwise names of the beneficiaries are also available in the Farmers Corner. \nCommon Service Centres (CSCs) have also been au\nthorized to assist the farmers in performing \nthe\n \nabove \ntasks.\n \n \n   \n \n \n           \nOn the advice of the Union Government, the States have organized Publicity/Awareness \nCamps to ensure \nexpeditious Aadhaar authentication of the data of the beneficiaries along with \nnew registrations and correction of already registered data. \n \n \n(e) &\n \n(f):    All eligible beneficiaries are entitled to be paid their benefits from the 4\n-\nmonthly \nperiod in which\n \ntheir registration has taken place, and hence they will not lose the benefit of any \ninstallment for procedural delays, except on account of their ineligibility once their registration \nhas been completed. \n \n \n \n                                                \n                                   \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_254": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LAW & JUSTICE \nDEPARTMENT OF LEGAL AFFAIRS \n***** \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.  *254 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11.03.2020 \n \nINTRODUCTION OF CPE/CLE FOR BAR COUNCIL MEMBERS \n \n*254. SHRI JASBIR SINGH GILL \n \nWill the Minister LAW AND JUSTICE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government is planning to introduce Continuing \nProfessional Education (CPE) and Continuing Legal Education \n(CLE) for members of Bar Council of India as has been done by a \nnumber of other countries to update professionals such as Chartered \nAccountants and Lawyers and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) the time frame within which the Government proposes to make it \ncompulsory for lawyers to obtain minimum number of hours/points \nfor CLE;  \n \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to implement the \nrecommendations of the Committee on Subordinate Legislation and \nLaw Commission of India in its 266th Report dealing with the \nAdvocates Act, 1961 that provides for a compulsory CLE through \neither Bar Council of India or any other  body prescribed by \nguidelines/ rules; and  \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes to amend the Advocates Act, \n1961 to implement the above recommendations and if so, the details \nthereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF LAW & JUSTICE, COMMUNICATIONS AND \nELECTRONICS & INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n \n(SHRI RAVI SHANKAR PRASAD) \n (a) to (d) A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d)  \nOF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *254  FOR \n11THMARCH , 2020 \n \n(a) & (b) This Ministry has conducted a study on Cont inuing Legal \nEducation (CLE) through National Law School  of India University \n(NLSIU) Bangalore and IIT Kharagpur. The Reports submitted by \nthem is under examination in consultation with the Bar Council of \nIndia. \n(c) & (d) The Law Commission of India in its 266th report pertaining \nto t he Advocates Act, 1961(Regulation of Legal Profession) has \nrecommended amendments therein. The Law Commission of India \nhas recommended amend Section 7 and 49 to provide for continuing \nlegal education fo r advocates and its monitoring. The \nRecommendations made by Law Commission of India in its 266 th \nreport are under consideration of this Department in consultation \nwith other stake holder. \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_255": " \nO.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 255 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 04.02.2020 \nDISHA COMMITTEE IN MAHARASHTRA \n \n255.  SHRI VINAYAK RAUT: \n \nWill the Minister of  RURAL DEVELOPMENT \n be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the progress of District Development Coordinat ion and Monitoring Committee \n(DISHA) in respect of Maharashtra State; and \n(b)  the details of meetings of DISHA Committee und er the chairmanship of Lok \nSabha Members of Parliament in respect of Maharashtra State? \n \n \nANSWER  \nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a): District Development Coordination and Monitori ng Committees (DISHA) have been \nformed in 36 districts of Maharashtra.  \n \n(b): The details of meetings of DISHA Committees un der the Chairmanship of Lok Sabha \nMembers of Parliament in respect of Maharashtra State are at Annexure .  \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred question No. 255 for reply on 04.02.2020 \n \nMeetings of  District Level DISHA Committees held in  Maharashtra  \nSl.No. District Number of DISHA Committee Meetings \n2016-17 2017-18 2018-19 2019-20 \n1 Ahmednagar 2  2 2 -  \n2 Akola 2  2 1 -  \n3 Amravati 2  3  - -  \n4 Aurangabad -  4  - -  \n5 Beed -  1  - -  \n6 Bhandara 2  -   - -  \n7 Buldhana 2  1  - -  \n8 Chandrapur 1  3 4 1 \n9 Dhule 1  1 1 -  \n10 Gadchiroli 2  3 1 -  \n11 Gondia 1  2  - -  \n12 Hingoli 3   -  - -  \n13 Jal Gaon 1  2 1 1 \n14 Jalna 1  1  - -  \n15 Kolhapur 1  2  -  - \n16 Latur 5  2 2 -  \n17 Nagpur - - - - \n18 Nanded 1  1  - -  \n19 Nandurbar 2  4 2 -  \n20 Nashik 2  -  -  -  \n21 Osmanabad 3  2 1 -  \n22 Mumbai city - - - - \n23 Mumbai Suburban - - - - \n24 Parbhani 2  2 3 -  \n25 Pune 3  3 2 -  \n26 Raigad 1   - -  -  \n27 Ratnagiri 2  2 -  -  \n28 Sangli 1  2 -  -  \n29 Satara - - - - \n30 Sindhudurg 2  2 1 -  \n31 Solapur 2  2 2 -  \n32 Thane 2  1 1 -  \n33 Wardha 1  2 -  -  \n34 Washim 2  -  -   - \n35 Yavatmal 1   - -  -  \n36 Palghar 2  1 -  -  \n  Total 55 53 24  2 \n  ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_256": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF DEFENCE \nDEPARTMENT OF DEFENCE PRODUCTION \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 256 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 11th March, 2020 \n \nBULLET PROOF JACKETS \n*256. SHRI CHANDRA PRAKASH JOSHI: \nWill the Minister of DEFENCE j{kk ea=h  \nbe pleased to state: \n (a) the scale of production of the Bullet Proof Jackets in the country; \n(b) the countries to which Bullet Proof Jackets made  in the country are being \nexported; and \n(c) the status of availability of Bullet Proof Jackets in the armed forces of the country? \n \n \nA    N    S    W    E    R \n \nMINISTER OF DEFENCE                                    (SHRI RAJNATH SINGH) \n      j{kk ea=h                  ¼Jh राजनाथ स िंह ) \n \n \n(a) to (c):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n******* \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED  TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( c) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 25 6 FOR ANSWER ON 11.03.2020 \nREGARDING ‘BULLET PROOF JACKETS’.  \n(a) Industrial Licenses have been issued to 15 companies for manufacturing Bullet \nProof Jackets (BPJs) having production capacity in the country of over 10 Lakh per \nannum to meet the domestic and export requirement. \n(b)  Bullet Proof Jackets are also being exported to 18 countries.  In view of the \nstrategic requirement, names of the countries are not disclosed.   \n(c)  BPJs are authorized as per laid down scale and are procured from time to time and \nmade available to soldiers as per specifications and authorization. \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS17_S3_U_257": " \n \n                                  \n \n \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nLOK  SABHA\n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.\n \n257\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE \n4\nTH \nFEBRUARY\n,\n \n20\n20\n \n \nCULTIVATION O\nF RABI CROPS\n \n \n257.\n  \nSHRI SHRIRANG APPA BARNE: \n \n         \nSHRI SUDHEER GUPTA:\n \n \n        \nSHRI BIDYUT BARAN MAHATO:\n \n \n        \nSHRI GAJANAN KIRTIKAR:\n \n \n        \nSHRI SANJAY SADASHIV RAO MANDLIK:\n \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE\n \nAND FARMERS WELFARE\n   \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं \u0000कसान क\u0000याण मं\u0000ी\n \nbe pleased to state:\n \n \n \n(\na\n)\n \n \nthe details of the total estimated acreage under cultivation of Rabi \nCrops during the \ncurrent season \nas compared to that of the last season, crop and State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(b) \n \nwhether the Government has estimat\ned the production of Rabi crops for the current \nseason and if so, the details thereof, crop and State\n-\nwise; \n \n \n(c) \n \nwhether the Government proposes to provide adequate incentives/assistances to the \nfarmers for sowing Rabi and Kharif grains and to procure th\neir produce at reasonable price and \nif so, the details thereof;\n \n \n(d) \n \nthe steps taken/being taken by the Government to enhance the production and acreage \nof crops in the country; and \n \n \n(e) \n \nthe steps taken by the Government to discourage the import of agri\ncultural produce in the \ncountry?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nM\nINISTER \n \nOF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE\n \n \nकृ\u0000ष\n \nएवं\n \n\u0000कसान\n \nक\u0000याण\n \nमं\u0000ी\n \n    \n(\nSHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR\n)\n \n \n(a) \n&\n \n(b):\n \nIt is early to assess the acreage and production of rabi crops for current rabi \nseason as sowin\ng is still in progress in some parts of the country. As per the release calendar of \nadvance estimates of crop production, the production and acreage of Rabi crops are normally \nreleased in mid February. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n(c) to (e):\n \nTo ensure remunerative price \nto farmers, the \nGovernment had increased Minimum \nSupport Prices (MSPs) for all mandated Kharif, Rabi and other Commercial crops with a return \nof atleast 50 percent over all India weighted average cost of production for the season 2018\n-\n19. \nGovernment has re\ncently increased the MSPs for all mandated Kharif and Rabi crops for 2019\n-\n20 season in line with the principle of providing atleast 50 percent return over all India weighted \naverage cost of production. Details of MSP, Cost and return over cost for year 201\n8\n-\n19 and \n2019\n-\n20\n \nare given at\n \nAnnexure\n.\n \n \n \nFurther, in order to increase production and acreage of agricultural crops in the country, \nGovernment of India is implementing through State Governments, several Crop Development \nSchemes/Programmes such as National\n \nFood Security Mission (NFSM), Rashtriya Krishi Vikas \nYojana (RKVY), Bringing Green Revolution to Eastern India (BGREI), National Mission for \nSustainable Agriculture (NMSA), Pradhan Mantri Krishi Sinchai Yojana, Soil Health Card etc. \nUnder these Schemes/Pr\nogrammes, funds are provided to States for implementation of State\n-\nspecific agricultural strategies including incentives to farmers for use of quality seeds, improve \nsoil health under Integrated Nutrient Management (INM), Integrated Pest Management (IPM), \nfarm mechanization, etc. The States are also provided support for creation of agricultural \ninfrastructure for optimal use of water and other natural resources.\n \n \nGovernment keeps close watch on import of agriculture produce in the country and take \nnecessary\n \nsteps from time to time so as to restrict cheap import of agriculture produce and \nprotect vulnerable farmers of the country by imposing Minimum Import Price, increasing import \nduties, imposing Quantitative restrictions on imports and restricting their imp\nort through \nlicensing as and when required.\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \n \n \nAnnexure\n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to Parts (c) to (e) of Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 257  due for reply on \n04.02.2020\n \nDetails of MSP, Cost and return over cost for y\near 2018\n-\n19 and 2019\n-\n20\n \n(Rs. per Quintal)\n \nSl. No.\n \nCommodity\n \n2018\n-\n19\n \n2019\n-\n20\n \nKHARIF  CROPS\n \nCost*\n \nMSP\n \n% \nReturn \nover \nCost\n \nCost*\n \nMSP\n \n% Return \nover \nCost\n \n1\n \nPADDY(Common)\n \n1166\n \n1750\n \n50.1\n \n1208\n \n1815\n \n50.2\n \n \n \n             \n(Grade A)^\n \n \n \n1770\n \n \n \n \n \n1835\n \n \n \n2\n \nJOWAR (Hy\nbrid)\n \n1619\n \n2430\n \n50.1\n \n1698\n \n2550\n \n50.2\n \n \n \n             \n(Maldandi) ^\n \n \n \n2450\n \n \n \n \n \n2570\n \n \n \n3\n \nBAJRA\n \n990\n \n1950\n \n97\n \n1083\n \n2000\n \n84.7\n \n4\n \nMAIZE\n \n1131\n \n1700\n \n50.3\n \n1171\n \n1760\n \n50.3\n \n5\n \nRAGI\n \n1931\n \n2897\n \n50\n \n2100\n \n3150\n \n50\n \n6\n \nARHAR(Tur)\n \n3432\n \n5675\n \n65.4\n \n3636\n \n5800\n \n59.5\n \n7\n \nMOONG\n \n4650\n \n6975\n \n5\n0\n \n4699\n \n7050\n \n50\n \n8\n \nURAD\n \n3438\n \n5600\n \n62.9\n \n3477\n \n5700\n \n63.9\n \n9\n \nCOTTON (Medium Staple)  \n \n3433\n \n5150\n \n50\n \n3501\n \n5255\n \n50.1\n \n \n \n               \n(Long Staple)^\n \n \n \n5450\n \n \n \n \n \n5550\n \n \n \n10\n \nGROUNDNUT IN SHELL\n \n3260\n \n4890\n \n50\n \n3394\n \n5090\n \n50\n \n11\n \nSUNFLOWER SEED\n \n3592\n \n5388\n \n50\n \n3767\n \n5650\n \n50\n \n1\n2\n \nSOYABEAN (Yellow)\n \n2266\n \n3399\n \n50\n \n2473\n \n3710\n \n50\n \n13\n \nSESAMUM\n \n4166\n \n6249\n \n50\n \n4322\n \n6485\n \n50\n \n14\n \nNIGERSEED\n \n3918\n \n5877\n \n50\n \n3960\n \n5940\n \n50\n \n \n \nRABI CROPS\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n1\n \nWHEAT\n \n866\n \n1840\n \n112.5\n \n923\